# Target C-II — parametric complete-rectangle tradeoff in the smallest leaf phase

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only quantitative strengthening of the repaired dual-tree rectangle theorem  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** repaired small-leaf complete-rectangle theorem; `P_V>0.0848-o(1)`; weighted center degree `>0.73D-o(D)`; center mass `<1.063D+o(D)`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The repaired rectangle theorem fixed a degree threshold `L=0.57` and obtained a complete
`0.003D x 0.35D` leaf/center crossing rectangle. The proof actually gives a one-parameter
family. Retaining the threshold instead of rounding it early gives larger rectangles and
lets later geometric arguments choose the aspect ratio they need.

Let `P` be the normalized retained smallest-leaf O--O pair weight, let `d_tau D` be the
number of exact-center trains crossing a leaf O--O train `tau`, and let `C_0D` bound the
total number of center trains. The certified inputs are

\[
P>p_0-o(1),\qquad p_0=0.0848,
\]

\[
{\sum_\tau w_\tau d_\tau\over\sum_\tau w_\tau}>a-o(1),
\qquad a=0.73,
\]

and

\[
C_0<c_0+o(1),\qquad c_0=1.063.
\]

Fix `0<L<a`. Call a retained leaf train `L`-good when `d_tau>L`. If `G_LD` is the
total retained weight of the good trains, weighted averaging gives

\[
aP<L(P-G_L)+c_0G_L+o(1),
\]

hence

\[
\boxed{G_L>g(L)-o(1),\qquad g(L):={(a-L)p_0\over c_0-L}.}
\tag{RT.1}
\]

Since every pair weight is at most one, there are more than `g(L)D-o(D)` distinct good
leaf O--O trains.

Apply the audited dual-tree construction to those good crosscuts. Their complement dual
is a tree. Double counting good-leaf/center incidences and using at most `c_0D+o(D)`
center trains shows that some center train crosses a simple dual-tree path containing

\[
\boxed{s(L)D-o(D),\qquad s(L):={Lg(L)\over c_0}}
\tag{RT.2}
\]

good leaf edges. Use that path as the backbone.

Now choose any fixed `alpha` with `0<alpha<s(L)`. Every backbone edge has center degree
`>LD-o(D)`. Intersections of center paths with the backbone are intervals. The same
consecutive-block count as in the repaired parent gives an `alpha D-o(D)` consecutive
leaf block with a common center neighborhood of density at least

\[
\boxed{
\beta(L,\alpha)
:=L-{\alpha c_0\over s(L)}
=L-{\alpha c_0^2(c_0-L)\over Lp_0(a-L)}.
}
\tag{RT.3}
\]

Whenever `beta(L,alpha)>0`, the current survivor contains a complete crossing rectangle

\[
\boxed{(\alpha D-o(D))\times(\beta(L,\alpha)D-o(D)).}
\tag{RT.4}
\]

No additional topology is used: the global good-leaf dual may branch, and only one
backbone path is ordered.

The exact `Fraction` verifier

`research/verify_target_c_small_leaf_rectangle_tradeoff_t1844.py`

checks several safe points on (RT.3):

\[
\boxed{>0.0031D\times>0.35D}\qquad(L=0.602),
\tag{RT.5}
\]

\[
\boxed{>0.0038D\times>0.30D}\qquad(L=0.591),
\tag{RT.6}
\]

\[
\boxed{>0.0051D\times>0.20D}\qquad(L=0.575),
\tag{RT.7}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{>0.0065D\times>0.10D}\qquad(L=0.562).
\tag{RT.8}
\]

For example, at `L=0.602`, exact arithmetic gives

\[
g(L)={6784\over288125}>0.0235453,
\]

\[
s(L)={4083968\over306276875}>0.0133342,
\]

and with `alpha=0.0031`,

\[
\beta(L,\alpha)
={23188392797\over65343488000}
>0.3548692>0.35.
\]

For a fixed `L`, the product `alpha beta(L,alpha)` is maximized at

\[
\alpha={Ls(L)\over2c_0},\qquad \beta={L\over2}.
\]

A nearby rational choice `L=0.591` already gives product density above `0.00114`.
This is only an optimization diagnostic; the aspect-ratio family (RT.3), rather than one
product optimum, is the useful statement for the next strip-order argument.

The theorem still does not bound reuse of one actual diameter edge across many
consecutive leaf cuts. Its purpose is to expose the strongest available rectangle at
whatever leaf/center aspect ratio that reuse argument requires.

**Disposition:** proved parametric quantitative strengthening; C-II and Level I remain open.
